feat: dual-license repo (CC BY 4.0 content + MIT code)

Split the existing single CC BY 4.0 license into a dual-license setup
so the framework content stays openly remixable while the code is
covered by a proper software license.

LICENSE files:
- LICENSE rewritten as a top-level explainer that defines which license
  applies to which parts of the repository
- LICENSE-CONTENT added (full CC BY 4.0 text, governs framework docs,
  templates, prompts, examples, sample projects, skill definitions)
- LICENSE-CODE added (full MIT text, governs installer scripts, hooks,
  CI workflows, plugin manifests, and other software)

SPDX headers:
- All shell scripts now carry # SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT directly
  under the shebang for automated license detection:
  install.sh, setup-repo.sh, test/smoke-test.sh, test/e2e/run-e2e.sh,
  test/e2e/scenarios/01-05-09*.sh, test/e2e/scenarios/02-03*.sh,
  .claude/statusline-command.sh, .claude/hooks/esf-session-status.sh

Metadata:
- CITATION.cff updated to list both CC-BY-4.0 and MIT
- CONTRIBUTING.md expanded with a contributor agreement explaining the
  dual license split and the SPDX header convention for new code files
- README.md adds a License section linking to LICENSE-CONTENT and
  LICENSE-CODE; footer updated to reflect both licenses

.gitignore:
- Add launch/ (private outreach and review drafts)
- Add .claude/settings.local.json (machine-specific settings)
- Add .qwen/ (other AI tool local state)
